      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "A novel delivery system for transdermal matrix patches of fentanyl has been developed. The new fentanyl tape consists of a backing layer, a drug-containing layer and a release liner that is originally made of rubber adhesive. This open-label prospective study investigated the pharmacokinetics and safety of the fentanyl tape in patients with cancer pain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The tape was applied to the patient's chest for 72 h, and serum samples were collected from 0 to 72 h. Fentanyl concentrations were determined using liquid chromatography with mass spectrometry. Pharmacokinetic parameters were calculated, and adverse events were evaluated according to the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (version 3.0)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Since 1 patient treated with 2.1 mg fentanyl was found to represent unique pharmacokinetic parameters by Shapiro-Wilk test, only 15 of the 16 study patients were investigated. Maximum serum concentrations (Cmax) and area under the curve (AUC)0-72 at 2.1 and 4.2 mg were 0.26 and 0.35 ng/ml, and 11.58 and 15.95 ng·h/ml, respectively. The Cmax and AUC0-72 tended to increase with dosage from 2.1 to 4.2 mg. There were no grade 3 toxicities, and only grade 1 ALT and AST elevations were observed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The novel delivery system of fentanyl may be a very promising approach to manage cancer pain clinically."
          }
        ]
      },
